planning_gate — implementation plan
===================================

The project has useful prose drift checks and static MUnit links, but no single
command establishes plan closure.  The current manifest is written before tests
run and can be generated without running them, so it is a verification matrix
input rather than execution evidence.

goal
----

Provide one host-side command:

    python3 -m tools.planning_gate done

A plan may be accepted as done only when its normalized static relationships
are complete, no closure blocker remains, and every required build and test
layer succeeds during that command invocation.

package
-------

Complete `tools/planning_gate/` as importable modules with one subcommand CLI:

    document.py     YAML frontmatter loading and schema validation
    labels.py       A/I/D/Q/T lifecycle and reference rules
    registry.py     generated registries and freshness
    matrix.py       producer merge and static coverage
    legacy.py       reviewed unlinked-debt ratchet
    refinements.py  OPEN/FOLDED checks
    commands.py     reusable build/test steps
    done.py         ordered completion composition
    __main__.py     plan, matrix, test-layer, theories, e2e, and done commands

Keep `tools/spec_gate.py` as a thin compatibility wrapper until callers move to
the package.  No package module parses arguments or exits during import.

static closure
--------------

For a plan to be done:

- every ACTIVE A/I is verified in every named layer;
- every T is covered by a distinct registered test in every named layer;
- every D dependency resolves;
- no Q is OPEN;
- no plan-owned spec refinement is OPEN;
- no missing relationship exists outside reviewed legacy debt;
- the plan has no remaining legacy-debt row when newly moving to done; and
- its ID, dependencies, modules, paths, and done command validate.

Static matrix/catalog generation never satisfies execution requirements.

execution closure
-----------------

`done` runs one fixed sequence against the same working tree:

1. Print the exact starting HEAD and all dirty paths.  Dirty state is a prominent
   warning for local human review, not an unconditional local failure.
2. Validate plan documents, labels, dependencies, refinements, and generated
   registry freshness.
3. Compile the production and test artifacts needed by registered producers.
4. Discover every producer and assemble/validate the verification matrix.
5. Run tooling-unit tests.
6. Run the complete Scala unit, heap, and bridge suites.
7. Build every registered ml-unit test theory/session.
8. Run the complete procedural Python e2e suite.
9. Confirm HEAD and tracked file content did not change during the run, then
   print one summarized verdict and exit nonzero if any step failed.

Every step is also an independently callable subcommand.  The done command does
not accept test filters or a reduced layer set.  It aggregates enough failure
information to identify the broken layer while preserving a nonzero result.

Functional correctness and performance budgets are reported as separate test
classes.  A required performance budget may still block done, but a timing
failure cannot be reported as if the functional assertion failed.

There is no custom persistent verification-result database in this plan.  The
human reviews the PR and the current command output.  CI integration is deferred
until the project decides how Isabelle, components, heaps, and caches run there.
Until then, the merge policy is human-enforced: do not merge or mark a plan done
unless the complete command is green.

implementation sequence
-----------------------

1. Refactor the existing `spec_gate.py` checks behind importable package APIs
   that accept explicit roots and inputs; preserve its current CLI behavior.
2. Add plan-format, label, matrix, legacy, and refinement checks from the
   prerequisite plans.
3. Inventory the exact authoritative build and execution command for every
   layer.  Reject a declared layer with no runner instead of silently skipping
   it.
4. Implement reusable subprocess steps with explicit argv, cwd, timeout,
   captured exit status, and interrupt propagation.  Do not evaluate plan-owned
   shell strings.
5. Implement `done` as composition of those steps and prohibit filters.
6. Add permanent, minimal synthetic-repository fixtures for static failure
   cases and fake-command fixtures for build/test failure propagation.  They
   are test data, not temporary live plans or additions to legacy debt.
7. Run the command against the real Flatpak Isabelle environment.  Keep the
   shutdown-drain regression in the required Scala suite; its current failure
   must make done red until WP1 fixes it.
8. Split the documentation TOC functional assertion from its performance budget
   before treating either result as a stable completion signal.

compatibility claims (canonical until v1 lands)
------------------------------------------------

A1 [tooling-unit]: One invocation is the only project command that may establish
    done; static metadata generation alone cannot.

A2 [tooling-unit]: A dirty working tree is reported with exact paths and left to
    explicit human review locally.

I1 [tooling-unit]: Each build, discovery, static-check, and test-layer step
    remains callable independently for diagnosis and agent iteration.

D1. CI integration is deferred; the host command is designed for later reuse
    without making CI a condition of this plan.

T1 [tooling-unit]: Static fixtures reject missing A/I verification, missing T
    coverage in any layer, open Q/refinement state, dangling D dependencies,
    unknown IDs, and unreviewed legacy debt.

T2 [tooling-unit]: Generating every discovery manifest without executing tests
    cannot make `done` succeed.

T3 [tooling-unit]: Each registered command-step failure makes `done` exit
    nonzero and identifies its failed step.

T4 [tooling-unit]: A filtered or reduced-layer invocation is rejected by the
    completion command while remaining available through diagnostic subcommands.

T5 [tooling-unit]: Dirty paths are printed; a changed HEAD or tracked content
    during execution invalidates the run.

T6 [scala-unit]: The existing shutdown-drain regression is selected by the full
    Scala suite and its failure propagates to `done`.

T7 [tooling-unit]: Documentation TOC classification fixtures distinguish
    functional and performance checks; test [scala-unit]: the checks produce
    distinct verdicts which the completion summary preserves.

T8 [tooling-unit]: An orchestration fixture requires every registered layer;
    test [scala-unit], [ml-unit], [heap], [bridge], and [e2e]: distinct cases
    execute in a real full run, which succeeds only when every layer is green.

non-goals
---------

- Installing or configuring Isabelle in CI.
- Persisting a signed or revision-pinned verification-result database.
- Using coverage percentages as evidence of completion.
- Automatically deciding whether a test semantically proves its linked claim.
